## 2. Design references

These projects informed the design. **No code was copied from them.** They are
recorded because the architecture answers problems they raised, and because that
provenance is the honest way to credit it.

### `pn1024/dsh-ppt-master`

https://github.com/pn1024/dsh-ppt-master — MIT.

A DSH plugin that packages ppt-master's skill. It demonstrated the
`ctx.skills.registerProvider()` mechanism and the `cordis.patch.yml` bundle
shape that this plugin's entry point follows. It **vendors** a full copy of
ppt-master under `skills/ppt-master/`; the difference here is deliberate — a
vendored copy cannot be fast-forwarded from upstream without a manual merge,
which is the problem this plugin exists to solve.

### `hongyi652/ppt-master-sci-fork`

https://github.com/hongyi652/ppt-master-sci-fork — MIT; original project
copyright Hugo He, fork changes copyright hongyi.

A fork of ppt-master adding MinerU document parsing and formula handling for
scientific PDFs. It is the **functional reference** for this plugin's SCI
skill: the decision to ingest research PDFs through MinerU rather than a text
layer, and the extraction of LaTeX into a reviewable manifest, both come from
what that fork proved out.

It implements those features by **patching files inside ppt-master**
(`skills/ppt-master/scripts/source_to_md/mineru_to_md.py`,
`scripts/extract_formulas.py`, `scripts/formula_display_policy.py`,
`scripts/latex_to_svg.py`, and a layout under `templates/layouts/`). That
approach cannot track upstream, because every upstream release is a merge
conflict.

This plugin reimplements the equivalent capability as an **independent skill**
outside the upstream tree, and deliberately diverges where the fork is now out
of date with upstream's own later work — see §3.

### `elvisw/ppt-master` references

Early documentation of the ppt-master lineage was consulted for historical
context. No content was carried across.

### A commercial chart library — design reference for `ppt-master-charts`

A commercial 200-page chart library distributed as a PowerPoint file with
accompanying generation prompts. It was supplied by the user of this repository
as a design reference and is **not redistributed, bundled, or committed** here.

**What was taken:** ideas only.

| Taken | Not taken |
| --- | --- |
| The taxonomy of five intents (structure / process / comparison / data / page) | No text from its prompt files |
| The names of generic diagram archetypes — pyramid, fishbone, swimlane, Venn, waterfall, and the rest | No slide file, SVG, or extracted geometry |
| The craft discipline (label every element in place; one readable statement per page; stated figures must reconcile with the data; one accent used sparingly; a minimum type size; native shapes only) | No page composition, and no image or asset of any kind |
| The idea of a style profile, abstracted *away* from it | Its palette is not used as a default — see below |

Diagram archetypes such as "SWOT", "fishbone" and "funnel" are generic
visual-communication concepts with no single author. The craft rules were
**rewritten to be style-independent**: the source states them alongside a
specific blue-gray palette, a specific typeface and a specific canvas, and none
of that is part of a rule.

**On its palette.** Its colour values are recorded once, in
`skills/ppt-master-charts/assets/style-profiles/blue-gray-business.json`, whose
`provenance.kind` is `reference-sample`. It exists to demonstrate what a
complete style profile looks like. It is explicitly **not** the house default —
`neutral-default.json` is — and the documentation states repeatedly that every
value in it is meant to be replaced. Colour values are facts and are not
protected; the file contains nothing else from the source.

If you are the rights holder and want this reference removed, delete the
`blue-gray-business.json` profile and the "Where these came from" section of
`skills/ppt-master-charts/references/craft-rules.md`. Nothing else depends on
it, by design.

## 3. Where this plugin deliberately differs from the sci-fork

Stated here so the divergence is a recorded decision rather than an omission.

| Area | sci-fork | This plugin | Why |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Upstream relationship | Fork; edits upstream files | Git submodule; edits nothing | Upstream releases stay a fast-forward |
| Delivery | One patched tree | Upstream + an independent skill | The overlay survives upstream redesigns |
| Formulas in slides | Renders LaTeX to SVG images | Routes to upstream's **native** formula markers (editable Office Math) | Upstream's `references/native-formula.md` forbids the picture branch: "Never substitute a PNG, flatten structural math into ordinary text, hand-write OMML, or leave raw LaTeX visible" |
| LaTeX → SVG | Core slide pipeline | Optional **preview only**, explicitly not a slide asset | Same rule; previews are a QA aid, not output |
| Formula sizing | Patches upstream's `svg_quality_checker.py` | Guidance in the skill's own references | Editing the checker would modify upstream |
| Academic layouts | Added under `templates/layouts/` inside the skill | Shipped as an **unregistered external workspace root** | Upstream supports `explicit` roots; registration would write into upstream |

### And a further divergence, from the chart reference

The chart library reference (`ppt-master-charts`) also hard-codes its style. This
plugin keeps its **forms** and its **craft**, and moves the style into a
swappable profile:

| Area | Reference chart library | This plugin | Why |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Style | Hard-coded throughout: one palette, one typeface, one canvas, stated as rules on every page | Extracted into `assets/style-profiles/*.json`, selected by precedence, with a colourless `neutral-default` fallback | A skill that bakes in one palette produces one look, then fights every brand it meets |
| Quantitative charts | Drawn as shape geometry on every page | Deferred to upstream's 33 `chart/<key>` references, which have one owner | Two catalogs drift; upstream's is current and states when native charts are available |
| Scale | 200 pages | ~200 archetype **keys** in a catalog, plus a validator | A skill needs coverage of relationships, not a slide count |
| Correctness | "Recheck the numbers yourself before output" (an instruction to the model) | `chart_plan.py` recomputes every stated figure from the page's own data | A rule the model must remember is a rule that gets skipped; a script does not forget |
| Editable claim | Shape geometry only — no native chart objects anywhere | Documents the native-vs-drawn trade-off and tells the author to choose | Shape geometry is right for a fixed published library and wrong when the audience wants the data |

